.Dd October 3, 2026
.Dt OPEN 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm open ,
.Nm openat ,
.Nm creat ,
.Nm close ,
.Nm close_range
.Nd open, create, or close a file descriptor
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In fcntl.h
.Ft int
.Fn open "const char *path" "int flags" "..."
.Ft int
.Fn openat "int dirfd" "const char *path" "int flags" "..."
.Ft int
.Fn creat "const char *path" "mode_t mode"
.In unistd.h
.Ft int
.Fn close "int fd"
.Ft int
.Fn close_range "unsigned int first" "unsigned int last" "int flags"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn open
opens
.Fa path
and returns a file descriptor.
A relative path is resolved from the current working directory.
.Fn openat
uses that directory when
.Fa dirfd
is
.Dv AT_FDCWD
(-100), or the directory referred to by
.Fa dirfd
otherwise.
An absolute path ignores
.Fa dirfd .
A directory descriptor is converted to a path; it does not bypass
search permission on ancestors.
.Pp
The access mode in
.Fa flags
is
.Dv O_RDONLY
(0),
.Dv O_WRONLY
(1), or
.Dv O_RDWR
(2), requesting read, write, or both permissions.
Search permission is required on ancestor directories.
File permissions are checked using the caller's effective user and
group IDs.
Effective user ID 0 overrides these permission checks.
.Pp
The following flags apply to files on mounted file systems:
.Bl -tag -width "O_DIRECTORY"
.It Dv O_CREAT
Create the file if it does not exist.
The additional argument is
.Vt mode_t
.Fa mode .
The parent must exist and permit writing.
On KRFFS, ext2 and tmpfs, the new file receives the caller's effective
user and group IDs and permission bits
.Li "mode & 07777 & ~umask" ,
or the parent's group when the parent is a set-group-ID directory, as on
Linux.
FAT32 and exFAT ignore the requested mode and report the mount's user
ID, group ID, and permissions 0777 with the mount mask removed.
Their read-only attribute removes write permission.
An existing file's ownership and mode are unchanged.
An existing directory returns
.Er EISDIR ,
as on Linux, since the flag asks for a file.
With
.Dv O_DIRECTORY ,
which asks for a directory, the call returns
.Er EINVAL
before the path is read, as Linux does since 6.4, unless
.Dv O_PATH
is given too.
.It Dv O_EXCL
With
.Dv O_CREAT ,
fail if the final name exists, including a symbolic link.
.It Dv O_TRUNC
Truncate an existing regular file to zero.
This requires write permission even with
.Dv O_RDONLY .
Truncating a directory is refused.
A nonprivileged truncation clears set-user-ID and clears set-group-ID
when group execute is set.
A file backing an attached loop device cannot be truncated.
.It Dv O_APPEND
Place each write at the current end of the file.
.It Dv O_NONBLOCK
Set nonblocking status on the open-file description.
Operations that support this status return
.Er EAGAIN
when they would wait.
.It Dv O_CLOEXEC
Close the descriptor on successful
.Xr execve 2 .
.It Dv O_DIRECTORY
Require an existing target to be a directory.
.It Dv O_NOFOLLOW
Do not follow a final symbolic link; return
.Er ELOOP
instead.
.It Dv O_PATH
Name the file without opening it; see
.Sx Path descriptors .
.El
.Pp
Other flag bits are not checked by the ordinary-file open handler and
have no effect there.
In particular, synchronous I/O flags do not cause writes to be flushed.
.Dv O_APPEND
and
.Dv O_NONBLOCK
belong to the open-file description and are shared by duplicated or
inherited descriptors.
The close-on-exec flag belongs to the individual descriptor.
.Pp
By default, a final symbolic link is followed.
With
.Dv O_CREAT ,
a dangling link causes its missing target to be created.
Path resolution follows at most 40 symbolic links.
The copied path, including any directory prefix, must fit 4096 bytes
with its terminating null byte.
.Pp
A path that ends in a slash, or whose last component is
.Pa \&.
or
.Pa \&.. ,
names a directory, as on Linux: the open acts as with
.Dv O_DIRECTORY ,
follows a final symbolic link even under
.Dv O_NOFOLLOW ,
and returns
.Er ENOTDIR
when the path reaches anything but a directory.
With
.Dv O_CREAT ,
which asks for a file, such a path creates nothing: it returns
.Er EISDIR
once the directories before its last name are walked, and a last
.Pa \&.
or
.Pa \&..
returns
.Er EEXIST
under
.Dv O_EXCL .
.Pp
On an existing ordinary target, the checks for
.Dv O_CREAT Ns | Ns Dv O_EXCL ,
a final symbolic link under
.Dv O_NOFOLLOW ,
a directory opened for writing, for truncation, or with
.Dv O_CREAT ,
and a non-directory under
.Dv O_DIRECTORY
precede the read-only mount and access checks.
A write open, creation, or truncation on a read-only mount returns
.Er EROFS .
The root directory can be opened for reading and directory enumeration.
.Pp
.Fn creat
is equivalent to
.Fn open
with
.Dv O_WRONLY Ns | Ns Dv O_CREAT Ns | Ns Dv O_TRUNC
and the specified
.Fa mode .
It truncates an existing regular file and refuses a directory,
including the root, with
.Er EISDIR .
.Ss FIFOs, sockets and device nodes
A FIFO, a socket file and a device node on a mounted file system have
no bytes of their own, and an open of one takes these checks, in this
order:
.Dv O_CREAT Ns | Ns Dv O_EXCL
returns
.Er EEXIST ,
.Dv O_DIRECTORY
returns
.Er ENOTDIR ,
and the node's own permission bits are checked for the access mode.
.Dv O_TRUNC
is ignored, and a read-only mount does not refuse an open for writing,
since nothing is written to the file.
.Pp
A FIFO opens as an end of the pipe every open of the file shares, made
at the first open and freed when no end is open.
The pipe is the file's own, whatever its name: a renamed FIFO keeps it,
and one unlinked while an end is open keeps it for that end, apart from
any FIFO made in its name meanwhile.
An open for reading waits for a writer and an open for writing for a
reader.
With
.Dv O_NONBLOCK ,
a reader does not wait and a writer with no reader fails with
.Er ENXIO .
.Dv O_RDWR
opens both ends and never waits.
The description holds the file and its mount as a regular file's does;
.Xr fstat 2
of the descriptor reports the pipe:
.Dv S_IFIFO ,
mode 0666 and the pipe's own inode number, where Linux reports the
FIFO file.
.Pp
A socket file returns
.Er ENXIO ,
as on Linux; it is reached by
.Xr connect 2 .
.Pp
A device node opens the
.Pa /dev
device of its kind whose number is the node's, as an open of the
.Pa /dev
path would, without that entry's permission check: the node's own
mode decides.
Major number 136 opens the pseudo-terminal slave whose index is the
minor number.
A number no present device has returns
.Er ENXIO ,
and a device node on a mount with
.Dv MS_NODEV
returns
.Er EACCES ,
as on Linux.
.Xr mknod 2
makes all three kinds.
.Ss Descriptors and close
An ordinary open starts at offset zero with a new open-file description.
On mounted KRFFS, opening a pathname during file relocation waits
for the move to finish before registering the open handle.
The handle follows later relocations, including after reuse of an old extent.
Descriptors are allocated from the lowest available number.
The process's table starts with 16 entries and grows up to 1024,
subject to its descriptor resource limit.
An open that finds no free number below the limit returns
.Er EMFILE ,
and one for which the kernel heap has no room to grow the table or for
the open-file description returns
.Er ENOMEM ,
as on Linux.
An open of a path takes its descriptor number before it resolves the
path, as Linux does, so an open that gets no number creates and
truncates nothing.
Until the open ends the number is reserved: a
.Fn close
of it by another thread sharing the table answers
.Er EBADF ,
a
.Xr dup2 2
onto it
.Er EBUSY ,
and a
.Xr fork 2
child does not inherit it.
An open of a
.Pa /dev
or
.Pa /proc
node, a device node or a socket file gives the number back before its
own allocation.
.Pp
.Fn close
releases
.Fa fd ,
including descriptors 0, 1, and 2, for reuse.
It drops the descriptor's socket reference and its reference to the
shared open-file description, and the
.Xr fcntl 2
record locks of the caller's descriptor table on the file, whichever
descriptor and whichever process sharing the table took them; the
description's own locks,
.Xr flock 2
locks and open file description locks, go with its last reference.
A pipe or terminal end is released when its last descriptor closes;
a mounted file's final close releases its per-open handle, node, and
mount reference.
On ext2, that release drains the mount's pending write batch.
A failed drain retains the pending inode state and error on the mount;
close still releases the descriptor, and fsync after reopening reports
the error.
Closing a framebuffer descriptor restores text mode only when the caller
owns the framebuffer and closes the last reference to that open-file
description, the references children inherited included.
The same releases occur on descriptor replacement by
.Xr dup2 2 ,
close-on-exec, and process exit.
.Pp
.Fn close_range
closes each open descriptor from
.Fa first
to
.Fa last ,
as
.Fn close
closes one; a number past the table's end is no descriptor, so
.Fa last
may be
.Dv ~0U .
With
.Dv CLOSE_RANGE_CLOEXEC
in
.Fa flags
it marks each close-on-exec instead, and with
.Dv CLOSE_RANGE_UNSHARE
it first gives a caller whose descriptor table threads share a copy of its
own, as
.Xr execve 2
does, and works on the copy.
Another flag, or a
.Fa first
past
.Fa last ,
is
.Er EINVAL .
.Pp
In a descriptor table that threads share, a close of a descriptor that a
call of another thread is using, one that looked it up since it began,
leaves the call what it had, as Linux's reference to the file does: the
number answers
.Er EBADF
and the table's record locks on the file go at once, and the other
releases above happen, and the number is free again, as that call ends.
A release may sleep, in a file system's release of the last reference to
a description among other places; until it ends, the number answers
.Er EBADF
to every thread, is not given to another descriptor, and
.Xr dup2 2
onto it gives
.Er EBUSY .
.Pp
Closing an ordinary descriptor for a file backing a loop device does
not detach the device.
The attachment retains its own file and mount references.
Closing a file opened on a loop-backed mount releases it in the same
way as a file on another mount.
.Ss Synthetic files and devices
The namespaces under
.Pa /dev
and
.Pa /proc
do not permit file creation.
Existing device entries open through their device operations and
enforce their access permissions.
An existing device leaf, including
.Pa /dev/ptmx ,
returns
.Er EEXIST
for
.Dv O_CREAT Ns | Ns Dv O_EXCL
before allocating a descriptor or pseudoterminal pair.
.Pp
Proc files contain a snapshot taken at open, except
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /oom_score_adj
and
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /mem ,
which reach the process at each read and write and may be opened for
writing, as
.Xr proc 5
describes.
The others refuse write access or
.Dv O_CREAT
with
.Er EACCES ,
after checking exclusive creation and
.Dv O_DIRECTORY ;
.Pa mem
refuses
.Dv O_CREAT
too, and
.Pa oom_score_adj
takes
.Dv O_CREAT
and
.Dv O_TRUNC
as a file that exists does.
Synthetic directories refuse write access and
.Dv O_CREAT
with
.Er EISDIR ,
but ignore
.Dv O_TRUNC
when opened read-only.
.Pp
The special paths
.Pa /fb
and
.Pa /mouse
open the framebuffer and mouse directly.
The kernel open handler ignores flags and mode for these paths, except
that neither device is a directory:
.Dv O_DIRECTORY
returns
.Er ENOTDIR ,
and so does a path that ends in a slash or in
.Pa \&.
or
.Pa \&..
after either name, before the device is touched.
With
.Dv O_CREAT ,
such a path follows the rule for
.Dv O_CREAT
above.
The framebuffer opens for reading and writing and is owned by one
process at a time; the mouse opens for reading and requires an
available mouse device.
.Ss Path descriptors
With
.Dv O_PATH
(010000000),
.Fn open
and
.Fn openat
return a descriptor that names the file and opens nothing, as on Linux.
Every other flag but
.Dv O_DIRECTORY ,
.Dv O_NOFOLLOW
and
.Dv O_CLOEXEC
is dropped, so nothing is created or truncated, and the access mode is
ignored.
The directories of the path need search permission; the file itself
needs none.
A final symbolic link is followed unless
.Dv O_NOFOLLOW
is given, and then the descriptor names the link.
A link under
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /fd
is not followed: without
.Dv O_NOFOLLOW
it returns
.Er ENOENT ,
as an ordinary open of one does, where Linux gives a descriptor on the
file the link names.
The file system's own open does not run: a device, an ext2 device inode
among them, is not opened, a
.Pa /proc
file takes no snapshot, and a mounted file system counts no open and no
release for the descriptor.
The descriptor keeps its file as an open descriptor does.
A mounted KRFFS volume, which keeps a node only while it is open,
counts a pin for the descriptor instead: an unlink, or a rename over the
file, leaves the file without a name until the descriptor's last close,
and
.Xr rmdir 2
of a directory it refers to returns
.Er EBUSY .
The special paths
.Pa /fb
and
.Pa /mouse
are not recognized.
.Pp
The descriptor serves
.Xr fstat 2 ,
.Xr fstatfs 2 ,
.Xr fchdir 2
on a directory,
.Fn fstatat
and
.Fn utimensat
with an empty path and
.Dv AT_EMPTY_PATH ,
the directory argument of the other
.Fn *at
calls,
.Xr dup 2 ,
.Xr dup2 2 ,
.Xr dup3 2 ,
the
.Xr fcntl 2
commands
.Dv F_DUPFD ,
.Dv F_DUPFD_CLOEXEC ,
.Dv F_GETFD ,
.Dv F_SETFD
and
.Dv F_GETFL ,
which reports
.Dv O_PATH ,
and
.Fn close .
.Xr read 2 ,
.Xr write 2 ,
.Xr lseek 2 ,
.Xr getdents64 2 ,
.Xr ioctl 2 ,
.Xr fsync 2 ,
.Xr ftruncate 2 ,
.Xr fchmod 2 ,
.Xr fchown 2 ,
.Fn utimensat
with a null path, and the other
.Xr fcntl 2
commands return
.Er EBADF ,
and
.Xr poll 2
reports the descriptor with
.Dv POLLNVAL .
Its entry in
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /fd
is the file's path, as an open descriptor's is; on a
.Pa /dev
or
.Pa /proc
node it is the path the descriptor was opened by, where Linux shows the
node's own path.
.Ss System call interface
The i386 system call numbers are 5 for
.Fn open ,
295 for
.Fn openat ,
8 for
.Fn creat ,
6 for
.Fn close ,
and 436 for
.Fn close_range .
.Fn open
takes path, flags, and mode in EBX, ECX, and EDX;
.Fn openat
takes directory descriptor, path, flags, and mode in EBX, ECX, EDX,
and ESI.
.Fn creat
takes path and mode in EBX and ECX;
.Fn close
takes the descriptor in EBX.
.Pp
The i386 musl implementation uses the legacy open system call for
.Fn open
and stdio file opens.
Its
.Fn creat
calls
.Fn open
with the flags described above.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn open ,
.Fn openat ,
and
.Fn creat
return a nonnegative descriptor on success.
.Fn close
returns 0.
On failure these functions return -1 and set
.Va errno .
The kernel interface returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/dirprobe.c
opens
.Pa /work
as a directory and uses its descriptor with
.Fn openat
to read
.Pa rel.txt
inside it.
It also checks append behavior and the mode passed to a creating
.Fn openat
call.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[ENAMETOOLONG]"
.It Bq Er EACCES
Search or file access permission was denied, creation lacked parent
write permission, a proc file was opened for writing or creation,
or a device node was opened on a mount with
.Dv MS_NODEV .
.It Bq Er EBADF
.Fn close
was given an invalid descriptor, or a relative
.Fn openat
path uses an invalid directory descriptor.
.It Bq Er EBUSY
Path resolution entered a mount being mounted or unmounted;
truncation was requested on a file backing a loop device;
the framebuffer belongs to another process; or a raw device's
exclusive-open or sector-claim rules prevent the open.
.It Bq Er EEXIST
Exclusive creation found an existing name, or a path whose last
component is
.Pa \&.
or
.Pa \&.. .
.It Bq Er EFAULT
The path is null or cannot be read from user memory, or an open or
creation call has no current process.
.It Bq Er EFBIG
An ext2 parent directory cannot grow further or a required block
exceeds the file system's addressing limit.
.It Bq Er EINTR
A signal arrived while an open of a FIFO waited for the other end.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Dv O_CREAT
and
.Dv O_DIRECTORY
were given together without
.Dv O_PATH ,
a file system rejects the new name, required attributes cannot be
obtained, truncation selects an ext2 object that is not a regular file,
or a FIFO was opened with the access mode 3;
.Fn close_range
was given a flag it does not take, or a
.Fa first
past
.Fa last .
.It Bq Er EIO
File-system I/O failed, the device was removed, or a pseudo-terminal
slave is still locked.
.It Bq Er EISDIR
A directory was opened for writing or with
.Dv O_CREAT ,
an ordinary directory was selected for truncation, or
.Dv O_CREAT
was given with a path that names a directory by its end.
.It Bq Er ELOOP
A final symbolic link was selected with
.Dv O_NOFOLLOW ,
or path resolution would follow more than 40 links.
.It Bq Er EMFILE
The process has no free descriptor below its limit.
.It Bq Er ENAMETOOLONG
A copied path, directory prefix, component, reconstructed directory
path, or symbolic-link expansion is too long.
.It Bq Er ENFILE
The kernel cannot allocate a synthetic-directory record, a
pseudo-terminal, or the pipe of a FIFO.
.It Bq Er ENODEV
The special mouse device was requested but no mouse is available.
.It Bq Er ENOENT
The path is empty, a required component does not exist, or a synthetic
entry or process no longer exists.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
A file-system node, per-open handle, proc snapshot, open-file description
or larger descriptor table cannot be allocated, or the copy of the table
.Dv CLOSE_RANGE_UNSHARE
asks for.
.It Bq Er ENOSPC
The file system has no room to create the file or its directory entry.
.It Bq Er ENOTDIR
A path component or relative-path base descriptor is not a directory,
or an existing target is not a directory under
.Dv O_DIRECTORY
or for a path that ends in a slash or in
.Pa \&.
or
.Pa \&.. .
.It Bq Er ENXIO
.Pa /dev/tty
was opened without a controlling terminal, the requested
pseudo-terminal has no open master, a socket file was opened, a device
node names no device present, or a FIFO was opened for writing with
.Dv O_NONBLOCK
and no reader.
.It Bq Er EOVERFLOW
The generated
.Pa /proc/disk
snapshot exceeds its size limit.
.It Bq Er EPERM
The file is immutable and write access or truncation was requested, or
it is append-only and write access was requested without
.Dv O_APPEND
or truncation with
.Dv O_TRUNC .
.It Bq Er EROFS
Creation or truncation requires a write on a read-only mount, write
access was requested on a read-only mount (the KRFFS boot root alone is
exempt), creation was requested in a synthetic namespace, or a required
write operation is not implemented.
.It Bq Er ESRCH
.Fn close
was called without a current process.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr dup 2 ,
.Xr fcntl 2 ,
.Xr flock 2 ,
.Xr getdents64 2 ,
.Xr mknod 2 ,
.Xr mount 2 ,
.Xr read 2 ,
.Xr umask 2 ,
.Xr write 2 ,
.Xr loop 4 ,
